namespace trion:
    use formal

    section "A composition stands for short sentences":
        A composition means a statement that joins short sentences.
        An expansion means a set of short sentences that replaces a composition.
        Each composition has exactly one expansion.
        A complex sentence means a composition that is exactly one sentence.
        A document structure means a composition that spans more than one sentence.
        Each complex sentence is a composition, and each document structure is a composition.

        [COMPOSITION-EXPANDS]
        The trion processor shall read each composition as the expansion of the composition.

        [COMPOSITION-NO-MEANING]
        The trion processor shall add no contract to the contracts of the expansion of a composition.

        [COMPOSITION-VALID]
        Each short sentence of an expansion shall be a statement of the trion language.

    section "A shared condition":
        A shared condition means a document structure that states exactly one condition for a group of statements.
        The expansion of a shared condition holds each statement with the condition.

        example SHARED-CONDITION:
            kind reading
            form contract
            input """
When the bell rings:
    [A] the API shall stop.
    [B] the API shall log the call.
"""
            expect "assume: Event(bell ring)"
            expect "guarantee: (Act(API|stop) ∧ ¬Forbid(API|stop) ∧ ¬Discouraged(API|stop))"
            expect "assume: Event(bell ring)"
            expect "guarantee: (Act(API|log call) ∧ ¬Forbid(API|log call) ∧ ¬Discouraged(API|log call))"

    section "A selection and a sequence stay one contract":
        A selection means a composition that offers alternatives.
        A sequence means a composition that orders actions.
        An action list means a composition that lists actions under exactly one modal verb.
        The operator of an action list is conjunction, inclusive choice, exclusive choice, or order.

        [COMPOSITION-KEPT]
        The trion processor shall read each selection and each sequence as exactly one contract.

        The guarantee of a selection is a disjunction.

        example ONE-OR-MORE:
            kind reading
            form contract
            input """
The API shall perform one or more of the following actions:
    accept the call;
    reject the call;
"""
            expect "assume: ⊤"
            expect "guarantee: ((Act(API|accept call) ∧ ¬Forbid(API|accept call) ∧ ¬Discouraged(API|accept call)) ∨ (Act(API|reject call) ∧ ¬Forbid(API|reject call) ∧ ¬Discouraged(API|reject call)))"

        An action list of the exclusive choice operator excludes each further alternative.

        example EXACTLY-ONE:
            kind reading
            form contract
            input """
The API shall perform exactly one of the following actions:
    accept the call;
    reject the call;
"""
            expect "assume: ⊤"
            expect "guarantee: (((Act(API|accept call) ∧ ¬Forbid(API|accept call) ∧ ¬Discouraged(API|accept call)) ∧ ¬(Act(API|reject call))) ∨ ((Act(API|reject call) ∧ ¬Forbid(API|reject call) ∧ ¬Discouraged(API|reject call)) ∧ ¬(Act(API|accept call))))"

        The guarantee of a sequence orders the completions of the actions.

        example IN-ORDER:
            kind reading
            form contract
            input """
The API shall perform the following actions in order:
    stop;
    log the stop;
"""
            expect "assume: ⊤"
            expect "guarantee: ((Act(API|stop) ∧ ¬Forbid(API|stop) ∧ ¬Discouraged(API|stop)) ∧ (Act(API|log stop) ∧ ¬Forbid(API|log stop) ∧ ¬Discouraged(API|log stop)) ∧ Before(API|stop, API|log stop))"

        An action list of the conjunction operator requires each action of the action list.

        example ALL:
            kind reading
            form contract
            input """
The API shall perform all of the following actions:
    stop;
    log the stop;
"""
            expect "assume: ⊤"
            expect "guarantee: ((Act(API|stop) ∧ ¬Forbid(API|stop) ∧ ¬Discouraged(API|stop)) ∧ (Act(API|log stop) ∧ ¬Forbid(API|log stop) ∧ ¬Discouraged(API|log stop)))"

    section "An exception narrows the assumption":
        An exception means a clause that begins with the word "unless".
        The assumption of a short sentence that has an exception holds the negation of that exception.

        example UNLESS:
            kind reading
            form contract
            input "When the door closes, the door shall become locked, unless the key is present."
            expect "assume: (Event(door close) ∧ ¬Val(key, present))"
            expect "guarantee: Val(door, locked)"
